WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,920
DDoS defense begins with knowing what must stay available.

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:14,817
That sounds obvious, but many incidents are made harder by unknown public services, forgotten domains, unowned APIs, old test systems, undocumented admin panels,

3
00:00:14,817 --> 00:00:20,618
or origins that were meant to sit behind an edge provider but remain directly reachable.

4
00:00:20,618 --> 00:00:23,675
An inventory is not paperwork for its own sake.

5
00:00:23,675 --> 00:00:29,006
It is the map responders use when time is scarce and the service is under pressure.

6
00:00:29,006 --> 00:00:32,769
A useful inventory covers more than public IP addresses.

7
00:00:32,769 --> 00:00:46,723
It includes domains, DNS zones, CDN front doors, WAF policies, API gateways, load balancers, login flows, customer portals, payment paths, status pages, monitoring endpoints, and third-party integrations.

8
00:00:46,723 --> 00:00:55,190
It also identifies administrative access paths and whether they depend on the same public edge as the customer-facing service.

9
00:00:55,190 --> 00:01:03,657
If responders cannot reach dashboards, management interfaces, or provider portals during an event, recovery becomes harder.

10
00:01:03,657 --> 00:01:09,301
Dependencies matter because availability is often constrained by the weakest path.

11
00:01:09,301 --> 00:01:19,806
A website may rely on DNS, certificate validation, an identity provider, a payment service, a queue, a cache, a database, a fraud service, and support tooling.

12
00:01:19,806 --> 00:01:25,529
A DDoS event against one public endpoint can expose hidden coupling in another system.

13
00:01:25,529 --> 00:01:36,504
The defensive inventory should show which dependencies are critical, which can fail gracefully, which have provider support, and which have alternate procedures.

14
00:01:36,504 --> 00:01:39,013
Ownership is part of the asset record.

15
00:01:39,013 --> 00:01:47,323
Each critical service should have an owner, an escalation path, a runbook, provider contacts, and a known business priority.

16
00:01:47,323 --> 00:01:58,063
The team should know which services are supposed to be public, which should be private, and which origins should accept traffic only through approved front doors.

17
00:01:58,063 --> 00:02:01,198
Unknown exposure creates incident confusion.

18
00:02:01,198 --> 00:02:07,313
Known exposure can be protected, monitored, tested, and improved before pressure arrives.
